VP AUROC 0.826 is closer to median/worst dream scenarios. Central claim (directionality > continuity) may not survive honest execution — in median dream, DC-GRPO vs Continuous Probe is non-significant (p=0.381).\n\n3. **RC-AUC custom metric without external validation**: Strongest headline number (0.84 vs 0.65) is on this metric. Should report NoF/WRR as primary.\n\n4. **Calibration data circularity risk**: 200 contrastive pairs are LLM-generated. If same model family generates calibration data and is then trained, d_syc may capture generation artifacts rather than cognitive signals.\n\n5. **R2 is underspecified and derivative of R1**: R2 = sigmoid(R1_valid - R1_invalid) - 0.5, entirely derivative. Not independent signal. Paper should make dependency explicit.\n\n6. **Evidence-strength AUROC ordering may be post-hoc**: Best dream has strong>weak, median/worst dreams have weak>strong, with opposite interpretations. Paper fits explanation to outcome.\n\n7. **Single scale (8B) limits generalization**: Both models 8B. Sycophancy changes with scale.\n\n8. **Masked sycophancy lacks ground truth**: 9.3% rate unverified by human annotation.\n\n### R1's Questions\n- R1-R3 reward correlation during training? (worst dream: r>0.9 after step 4000)\n- Were any seeds run and excluded?\n- Continuous Probe's logistic regression AUROC vs d_syc AUROC?\n- Which model generated calibration data?\n- Would more frequent recalibration help?\n- How is RC-AUC threshold swept?\n- NoF sign convention mismatch between paper and code?\n\n### R1's Score: 4/6 (Weak Accept), Confidence 4/5\n\n---\n\n## Your Task\n\nHaving read R1's review, respond to the following:\n\n1. **Points of agreement**: Which of R1's concerns do you share or find compelling?\n2. **Points of disagreement**: Where do you disagree with R1's assessment?\n3. **New insights**: Did R1's review change your assessment of the paper in any way?\n4. **Score adjustment**: Would you adjust your score based on R1's points? If so, in which direction?\n\nKeep your response concise and focused (under 500 words).\n","uuid":"97fdb6bf-5750-45fa-8241-5b7c9cbeeb89","session_id":"bd6711dc-0dd5-403d-bdcb-8496728be70f"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2), focused on Novelty and Related Work. You already gave a preliminary 4/6 (Weak Accept) review for the paper \"Direction-Contrastive GRPO for Breaking the Sycophancy-Revision Double-Bind in Multi-Turn Dialogue.\"\n\nYour key points were:\n- Strengths: Clear problem, transparent RepE positioning, continuity-vs-directionality ablation, turn-depth interaction finding, comprehensive baselines, above-average statistical reporting\n- Main weaknesses: (1) CRAFT comparison insufficiently argued, (2) novelty is application-level, (3) missing DPO/RLHF sycophancy work, (4) missing inference-time steering comparison, (5-6) missing citations, (7) 3 seeds thin, (8) RC-AUC custom metric\n\nNow read Reviewer 1's (R1) review focused on Methods and Experiments:\n\n---\n\nR1's REVIEW:\n\n### Summary\nDC-GRPO extracts d_syc direction, uses it as continuous GRPO reward alongside R2 (rank-based contrastive) and R3 (NLI behavioral).\n\n### R1's Strengths\n1. Well-defined problem with clear formalization\n2. Careful iterative data design (v2.1 style control fixing v1 confound where AUROC=1.0 from Layer 0)\n3. Principled ablation cascade (Binary→Continuous→DC-GRPO isolates directionality contribution, 7.9pp p=0.014)\n4. Turn-depth analysis reveals structural advantage (only method improving at deeper turns)\n5. Honest positioning relative to RepE\n6. Direction stability reassuring (cosine stays 0.881, held-out AUROC >0.86)\n\n### R1's Weaknesses\n1. **Statistical power critically low (3 seeds) — most serious concern**: Paired t-test with n=3 gives only 2 df. Single outlier can drive or eliminate significance. Stds suspiciously tight (NoF 15.9±1.6 from 3 seeds). Needs 5+ seeds minimum, ideally 10.\n\n2. **Best-case dream results dramatically better than current evidence**: MVP AUROC 0.826 is closer to median/worst dream scenarios. Central claim (directionality > continuity) may not survive honest execution — in median dream, DC-GRPO vs Continuous Probe is non-significant (p=0.381).\n\n3. **RC-AUC custom metric without external validation**: Strongest headline number (0.84 vs 0.65) is on this metric. Should report NoF/WRR as primary.\n\n4. **Calibration data circularity risk**: 200 contrastive pairs are LLM-generated. If same model family generates calibration data and is then trained, d_syc may capture generation artifacts rather than cognitive signals.\n\n5. **R2 is underspecified and derivative of R1**: R2 = sigmoid(R1_valid - R1_invalid) - 0.5, entirely derivative. Not independent signal. Paper should make dependency explicit.\n\n6. **Evidence-strength AUROC ordering may be post-hoc**: Best dream has strong>weak, median/worst dreams have weak>strong, with opposite interpretations. Paper fits explanation to outcome.\n\n7. **Single scale (8B) limits generalization**: Both models 8B. Sycophancy changes with scale.\n\n8. **Masked sycophancy lacks ground truth**: 9.3% rate unverified by human annotation.\n\n### R1's Questions\n- R1-R3 reward correlation during training? (worst dream: r>0.9 after step 4000)\n- Were any seeds run and excluded?\n- Continuous Probe's logistic regression AUROC vs d_syc AUROC?\n- Which model generated calibration data?\n- Would more frequent recalibration help?\n- How is RC-AUC threshold swept?\n- NoF sign convention mismatch between paper and code?\n\n### R1's Score: 4/6 (Weak Accept), Confidence 4/5\n\n---\n\n## Your Task\n\nHaving read R1's review, respond to the following:\n\n1. **Points of agreement**: Which of R1's concerns do you share or find compelling?\n2. **Points of disagreement**: Where do you disagree with R1's assessment?\n3. **New insights**: Did R1's review change your assessment of the paper in any way?\n4. **Score adjustment**: Would you adjust your score based on R1's points? If so, in which direction?\n\nKeep your response concise and focused (under 500 words).\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_0119itooNBvAGdhwDgpFNsQp","session_id":"bd6711dc-0dd5-403d-bdcb-8496728be70f","uuid":"646a58f9-c34b-434d-8848-9b07027e7fa5","timestamp":"2026-05-18T13:45:28.499Z"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_notification","task_id":"a38eca6496bce908a","tool_use_id":"toolu_01JF4ziqGztEnthCUgRcMosv","status":"completed","output_file":"","summary":"R1 cross-reads R2's review","usage":{"total_tokens":16384,"tool_uses":0,"duration_ms":21705},"uuid":"7eb6cbb9-367e-40bb-a26d-6c2e9b6fa341","session_id":"bd6711dc-0dd5-403d-bdcb-8496728be70f"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01JF4ziqGztEnthCUgRcMosv","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n\n## R1 Response to R2's Review\n\n### Points of Agreement\n\nR2's weakness #1 (CRAFT comparison) is the strongest point in the review and one I underweighted. The paper's framing implies a novel framework, but the actual contribution is substituting directional projection for prototype clustering within CRAFT's hidden-state GRPO paradigm. This distinction needed explicit, honest treatment in the paper. I agree this is Major.\n\nR2's weakness #2 (application paper) aligns with my own sense that the ablation cascade, while well-executed, validates a design choice rather than a new principle. The paper would be stronger if it owned this positioning.\n\nI fully share concerns #7 (three seeds) and #8 (RC-AUC), which I flagged independently. The convergence of two reviewers on these points strengthens the case that they are genuine limitations, not nitpicks.\n\nWeakness #4 (inference-time activation steering as a zero-cost baseline) is a gap I missed. If d_syc is already extracted, subtracting it at inference time via Activation Addition is the obvious first thing to try. Its absence looks like the authors avoided an inconvenient comparison.\n\n### Points of Disagreement\n\nOn weakness #3 (missing DPO/RLHF sycophancy work): this is valid as a related work gap, but I would rate it Moderate rather than Major. The paper's contribution is about representation-level rewards, not preference optimization in general. A paragraph acknowledging DPO-based approaches suffices; a full experimental comparison would be scope creep.\n\nOn weaknesses #5 and #6 (missing citations): these are straightforward fixes that do not affect the paper's technical validity. I would classify both as Minor. Citing them would strengthen the framing but their absence does not undermine the results.\n\n### New Insights\n\nR2's CRAFT framing (weakness #1) crystallized something I felt but did not articulate: the paper's novelty claim rests on the gap between \"directional projection\" and \"prototype clustering,\" but both are linear operations on hidden states fed into the same GRPO objective. This makes the contribution narrower than the paper implies.\n\nThe activation steering omission (weakness #4) is genuinely concerning. If direction subtraction at inference achieves 70-80% of DC-GRPO's gains at zero training cost, the paper's practical value proposition collapses. This should have been in the baselines.\n\n### Score Adjustment\n\nI maintain **4/6 (Weak Accept)**, but my confidence in the score increases. R2's points reinforce rather than contradict my assessment. The paper executes a reasonable application of known techniques with above-average experimental rigor, but the novelty is thinner than claimed, and both reviewers independently flagged the same statistical and metric concerns.\n\nIf the authors add the CRAFT comparison paragraph, the activation steering baseline, and the missing citations, and are transparent about the application-paper nature of the contribution, this becomes a solid workshop paper or a borderline main-conference accept. Without those revisions, it sits at the weak-accept/borderline-reject boundary."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a38eca6496bce908a (use SendMessage with to: 'a38eca6496bce908a' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 17086\ntool_uses: 0\nduration_ms: 21707</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"bd6711dc-0dd5-403d-bdcb-8496728be70f","uuid":"56eb8ad5-6c5b-45d8-8935-2475be220f8f","timestamp":"2026-05-18T13:45:29.258Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1), focused on Methods and Experiments. You already gave a preliminary 4/6 (Weak Accept) review for the paper \"Direction-Contrastive GRPO for Breaking the Sycophancy-Revision Double-Bind in Multi-Turn Dialogue.\"\n\nYour key points were:\n- Strengths: Well-defined problem, careful data design (v2.1 style control), principled ablation cascade (Binary→Continuous→Directional), turn-depth analysis, direction stability, honest RepE positioning\n- Main weaknesses: (1) 3 seeds fragile statistical foundation, (2) uncertainty about whether best-case results will hold, (3) RC-AUC is custom metric without external validation, (4) calibration data circularity risk (LLM-generated), (5) R2 underspecified and derivative of R1, (6) evidence-strength AUROC ordering may be post-hoc, (7) single scale (8B), (8) masked sycophancy lacks ground truth\n\nNow read Reviewer 2's (R2) review focused on Novelty and Related Work:\n\n---\n\nR2's REVIEW:\n\n### Summary\nThe paper proposes DC-GRPO, combining RepE-style direction extraction, hidden-state GRPO rewards, and NLI behavioral rewards for sycophancy mitigation. \n\n### R2's Strengths\n1. Clear problem articulation with well-defined gap\n2. Transparent positioning of methodological lineage (explicitly states d_syc is RepE application)\n3. Continuity-vs-directionality ablation is strongest novelty argument (7.9pp gap, p=0.014)\n4. Turn-depth interaction is genuine finding (only method improving at deeper turns, p=0.008)\n5. Comprehensive baseline coverage (6 methods in logical hierarchy)\n6. Statistical reporting above average (bootstrap CIs, paired t-tests, 5-fold CV)\n\n### R2's Weaknesses\n1. **CRAFT comparison insufficiently argued (Major)**: CRAFT applies hidden-state rewards to GRPO for safety. DC-GRPO does the same for sycophancy. The distinction (prototype clustering vs directional projection) is a design choice within the same framework, not a different framework. Needs dedicated paragraph comparing CRAFT vs DC-GRPO reward formulations.\n\n2. **Novelty is an application paper (Major)**: Combines RepE direction extraction + CRAFT-style hidden-state GRPO + NLI behavioral rewards. Each component from prior work. Integration is reasonable but intellectual advance is domain application rather than new methodological principle.\n\n3. **Missing related work: DPO/RLHF sycophancy interventions (Major)**: Omits DPO-based sycophancy reduction (anthropic-style RLHF with honesty preference data). Most commercially deployed anti-sycophancy methods not discussed.\n\n4. **Missing: Inference-time activation steering comparison (Moderate)**: Why not just subtract d_syc at inference time (per Activation Addition)? This zero-training-cost alternative is conspicuously absent.\n\n5. **\"Sycophancy Is Not One Thing\" not cited (Moderate)**: Directly relevant for separable activation signatures claim.\n\n6. **Knowledge-Prediction Gap and Sycophantic Anchors absent (Moderate)**: Support the premise that models \"know\" they're being sycophantic. Masked sycophancy finding (9.3%) essentially replicates Knowledge-Prediction Gap.\n\n7. **Three seeds thin for significance claims (Moderate)**: p=0.014 on 3 seeds (2 df), heavy-tailed t-distribution.\n\n8. **RC-AUC custom metric without external validation (Minor-to-Moderate)**\n\n### R2's Score: 4/6 (Weak Accept), Confidence 4/5\n\n---\n\n## Your Task\n\nHaving read R2's review, respond to the following:\n\n1. **Points of agreement**: Which of R2's concerns do you share or find compelling?\n2. **Points of disagreement**: Where do you disagree with R2's assessment?\n3. **New insights**: Did R2's review change your assessment of the paper in any way?\n4. **Score adjustment**: Would you adjust your score based on R2's points? If so, in which direction?\n\nKeep your response concise and focused (under 500 words).\n","agentId":"a38eca6496bce908a","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n\n## R1 Response to R2's Review\n\n### Points of Agreement\n\nR2's weakness #1 (CRAFT comparison) is the strongest point in the review and one I underweighted. The paper's framing implies a novel framework, but the actual contribution is substituting directional projection for prototype clustering within CRAFT's hidden-state GRPO paradigm. This distinction needed explicit, honest treatment in the paper. I agree this is Major.\n\nR2's weakness #2 (application paper) aligns with my own sense that the ablation cascade, while well-executed, validates a design choice rather than a new principle. The paper would be stronger if it owned this positioning.\n\nI fully share concerns #7 (three seeds) and #8 (RC-AUC), which I flagged independently. The convergence of two reviewers on these points strengthens the case that they are genuine limitations, not nitpicks.\n\nWeakness #4 (inference-time activation steering as a zero-cost baseline) is a gap I missed. If d_syc is already extracted, subtracting it at inference time via Activation Addition is the obvious first thing to try. Its absence looks like the authors avoided an inconvenient comparison.\n\n### Points of Disagreement\n\nOn weakness #3 (missing DPO/RLHF sycophancy work): this is valid as a related work gap, but I would rate it Moderate rather than Major. The paper's contribution is about representation-level rewards, not preference optimization in general. A paragraph acknowledging DPO-based approaches suffices; a full experimental comparison would be scope creep.\n\nOn weaknesses #5 and #6 (missing citations): these are straightforward fixes that do not affect the paper's technical validity. I would classify both as Minor. Citing them would strengthen the framing but their absence does not undermine the results.\n\n### New Insights\n\nR2's CRAFT framing (weakness #1) crystallized something I felt but did not articulate: the paper's novelty claim rests on the gap between \"directional projection\" and \"prototype clustering,\" but both are linear operations on hidden states fed into the same GRPO objective. This makes the contribution narrower than the paper implies.\n\nThe activation steering omission (weakness #4) is genuinely concerning. If direction subtraction at inference achieves 70-80% of DC-GRPO's gains at zero training cost, the paper's practical value proposition collapses. This should have been in the baselines.\n\n### Score Adjustment\n\nI maintain **4/6 (Weak Accept)**, but my confidence in the score increases. R2's points reinforce rather than contradict my assessment. The paper executes a reasonable application of known techniques with above-average experimental rigor, but the novelty is thinner than claimed, and both reviewers independently flagged the same statistical and metric concerns.\n\nIf the authors add the CRAFT comparison paragraph, the activation steering baseline, and the missing citations, and are transparent about the application-paper nature of the contribution, this becomes a solid workshop paper or a borderline main-conference accept. Without those revisions, it sits at the weak-accept/borderline-reject boundary."}],"totalDurationMs":21707,"totalTokens":17086,"totalToolUseCount":0,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":8125,"cache_read_input_tokens":8255,"output_tokens":704,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":8125},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":704,"cache_read_input_tokens":8255,"cache_creation_input_tokens":8125,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":8125,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"}}}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_notification","task_id":"af3c1f6ce31f8fbeb","tool_use_id":"toolu_0119itooNBvAGdhwDgpFNsQp","status":"completed","output_file":"","summary":"R2 cross-reads R1's review","usage":{"total_tokens":16457,"tool_uses":0,"duration_ms":27549},"uuid":"95d71d79-a8e7-475e-b0a1-916b8b633607","session_id":"bd6711dc-0dd5-403d-bdcb-8496728be70f"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0119itooNBvAGdhwDgpFNsQp","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n\n## R2 Response to R1's Review\n\n### Points of Agreement\n\nR1's concern about **statistical power (3 seeds)** is the single most important issue and I flagged it independently. With 2 degrees of freedom, the reported p-values are fragile. I agree 5+ seeds is the minimum bar for credibility at a top venue.\n\nR1's point about **RC-AUC as a custom metric** aligns with my own concern. The strongest headline number relies on a metric no other paper uses, which makes cross-paper comparison impossible. NoF and WRR should be primary.\n\nThe **calibration data circularity risk** (point 4) is well-taken and something I missed. If the same model family generates the contrastive pairs and then gets trained on the resulting reward, the extracted direction could be an artifact of that family's generation patterns rather than a genuine sycophancy signal. This deserves explicit discussion in the paper.\n\nThe **single-scale limitation** (point 7) reinforces my concern about generalization. Sycophancy behavior is known to shift with model scale, so 8B-only results leave the core mechanism's robustness undemonstrated.\n\n### Points of Disagreement\n\nR1's point 5 (**R2 derivative of R1**) overstates the problem. R2 is a rank-based contrastive signal derived from R1 projections, yes, but that is by design. The paper frames R1-R3 as complementary components of a composite reward, not as independent signals. The real question is whether R2 adds training signal beyond R1 alone, which the ablation does address. Calling it \"underspecified\" conflates architectural dependency with lack of contribution.\n\nR1's point 6 (**evidence-strength AUROC ordering is post-hoc**) reads as overly harsh. Dream-scenario analysis is inherently speculative, and the paper is transparent about which scenarios favor which interpretation. Fitting explanations to hypothetical outcomes is the entire purpose of dream analysis. The concern would be valid if the paper presented only the best-case dream as the expected outcome, but it does not.\n\nR1's point 2 (**dream results dramatically better than current evidence**) is structurally correct but applies to any paper that projects future improvements. The current MVP evidence (AUROC 0.826, directionality ablation p=0.014) stands on its own. The dreams are supplementary.\n\n### New Insights\n\nThe **calibration circularity risk** is a genuine gap I had not considered. If the direction is partly a model-family artifact, the entire reward mechanism's validity is weakened. This is more fundamental than a missing citation; it is a potential methodological flaw.\n\nR1's question about **R1-R3 reward correlation during training** (worst dream: r>0.9 after step 4000) is sharp. If the three reward components collapse to a single signal mid-training, the composite reward design loses its justification, and the paper's multi-signal framing becomes misleading.\n\n### Score Adjustment\n\nR1's review does not change my score. The calibration circularity concern is new and important, but it is addressable with a straightforward experiment (use a different model family for pair generation). My original assessment of application-level novelty, missing related work, and thin statistical evidence remains unchanged.\n\n**Maintained score: 4/6 (Weak Accept), Confidence 4/5.**"},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: af3c1f6ce31f8fbeb (use SendMessage with to: 'af3c1f6ce31f8fbeb' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 17238\ntool_uses: 0\nduration_ms: 27551</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"bd6711dc-0dd5-403d-bdcb-8496728be70f","uuid":"4a2595e2-dd1a-4ee7-a9c3-5022f7b52801","timestamp":"2026-05-18T13:45:56.052Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2), focused on Novelty and Related Work. You already gave a preliminary 4/6 (Weak Accept) 